2. Existing structure to retain and generalize

Source/Biology/REMORA_Biology.H currently defines none/fennel selection,
FennelParameters, optional tracer counts, and Fennel component mappings.
Source/Biology/REMORA_Biology.cpp combines parsing/metadata helpers and the
native Fennel update in REMORA::advance_biology.

The state already has a runtime layout:
  [temperature, salinity, passive dyes, active biology tracers]
  Bio_comp = Tracer_comp + nscalar
  ncons = Tracer_comp + nscalar + nbio
  Tracer_comp = 2

Keep those definitions and the distinction between dyes and biology. The
selected ecosystem defines its tracer inventory. Modules may request extra
advected tracers through the same inventory mechanism, but do not require
them. Nonadvected module state must not be inserted into the tracer block.

Source/REMORA.cpp currently loads Fennel parameters for any active biology
and constructs names through a Fennel-dependent helper. Generalize these
entry points to the abstract interface; do not add a parameter member or
metadata switch to REMORA for every new ecosystem or module.

Source/TimeIntegration/REMORA_advance_3d.cpp calls advance_biology after the
GLS corrector. Tracer RHS advection, conversion of thickness-weighted state,
vertical mixing, and nudging operations occur subsequently. Keep this actual
placement and numerical contract rather than treating the hook as a generic
end-of-step concentration update.

Source/Initialization/REMORA_init.cpp, REMORA_prob_common.H, and
Exec/REMORA_Prob.H/.cpp have analytic biology hooks taking FennelParameters.
Replace the permanent interface with a model-neutral initialization context.
Retain a temporary adapter for existing Fennel problem initializers if needed.
Do not add a new problem-base virtual signature for each ecosystem.

NetCDF biology initialization already consumes cons_names and an alias of
the biology block. Boundary, river, climatology, and many scalar controls
also use names and ncons. Reuse them, including the full-domain high-resolution
initialization followed by average-down.

Preserve akt_comp's current use of salinity vertical diffusivity for biology
and dye fields unless different physics is explicitly required. Keep per-tracer
horizontal diffusivity configuration. Plot-variable expansion and checkpoint
identity require small generalizations; scalar transport algorithms do not
need an ecosystem-specific rewrite.

3. Separate ecosystem implementations and inventories

Use ROMS ecosystem options as scientific references, not as interchangeable
sets of identically named variables. Their implementations separate source/sink
equations, parameters, parameter input, variable metadata, and output of the
configuration. Keep those responsibilities in each concrete class using
REMORA's C++ and ParmParse conventions.

Fennel in the examined baseline has seven base tracers:
  NO3, NH4, chlorophyll, phytoplankton, zooplankton, LdetritusN, SdetritusN.
Optional PO4, carbon, oxygen, river organic pools, and ODU change the layout;
the fully enabled inventory has 16 fields.

The examined ROMS ECB module declares 18 fields, in order:
  NO3, NH4, chlorophyll, phytoplankton, zooplankton, LdetritusN,
  SdetritusN, semilabileDON, PO4, LdetritusC, SdetritusC, TIC,
  alkalinity, semilabileDOC, oxygen, refractoryDON, refractoryDOC, calcium.

ECB includes additional dissolved-organic pathways, exudation, carbon excess
uptake, attenuation, and bottom-process parameterizations. Some registered
fields are functional or transported without a direct local reaction in the
examined kernel. Do not invent transformations based only on field names.
For example, Fennel's river organic pools are not automatically equivalent
to ECB's semilabile dissolved organic pools.

The examined NEMURO module has 11 fields: small and large phytoplankton;
small, large, and predator zooplankton; NO3; NH4; PON; DON; silicate; opal.
Its food web, silicon cycle, and sinking fields differ from Fennel and ECB.
Do not allocate unused Fennel fields merely to force it into a shared layout.

Each concrete ecosystem owns its equations, parameters, optional features,
reference constraints, integration order, tracer definitions, and scientific
field meanings. The base class defines how REMORA uses an ecosystem; it does
not prescribe one common ecosystem or a union of all possible parameters.

ROMS reference description:
  https://github.com/myroms/roms/wiki/ROMS-Kernels

4. Abstract Biology interface and ownership

Proposed host-side ownership:

  REMORA
    owns BiologySystem
      owns unique_ptr<Biology> ecosystem
      owns vector<unique_ptr<BiologyModule>> optional_modules

  Biology is an abstract base class
    FennelBiology derives from Biology
    ECBBiology derives from Biology
    NemuroBiology derives from Biology

  BiologyModule is a separate abstract extension interface
    User implementations derive from BiologyModule when needed

These names are schematic. Do not create empty ECB/NEMURO implementations
that appear selectable before their kernels exist. A factory should reject
unimplemented models. The none option can use a null ecosystem with a single
common no-biology guard.

Keep selection in a small construction factory. A simple factory switch is
acceptable; eliminate recurring switches from parameter loading, name/count
queries, initialization, stepping, and I/O integration. Dynamic library
loading and a runtime plugin registry are not required for this capability.
New classes can initially be compiled and registered in the executable.

Illustrative Biology responsibilities, not compile-ready declarations:

  virtual ~Biology()
  model_id() and schema_version()
  configure(parameter_prefix)
  tracer_definitions() and field_capabilities()
  bind_layout(frozen_layout)
  initialize(initialization_context)
  prepare_reference_workspace(advance_context, workspace)
  advance_ecosystem(advance_context, workspace)
  configuration_metadata() and supported_backends()

Required methods are public and pure virtual where an implementation is
needed. Lifecycle calls occur on the host. Parameter structures are owned by
the concrete ecosystem, not by the base class or a growing REMORA parameter
union. The base class has a virtual destructor and no device-side virtual
dispatch requirement.

BiologySystem coordinates layout, module binding, scheduling, state lifecycle,
and final coupling to REMORA. It must not contain Fennel growth or ECB chemistry
equations. Keep the interface small; use ordinary descriptors and context
objects instead of one virtual method for every tracer or process.

The permanent REMORA::advance_biology wrapper delegates to BiologySystem.
An initial FennelBiology adapter may call the existing native kernel unchanged
while ownership and metadata are migrated. Remove the temporary dependency on
REMORA-owned fennel_params after the class owns the canonical parameter state;
avoid two independently mutable copies of the same configuration.

5. Generic optional module interface

A module contributes additional processes or state while the selected Biology
class remains responsible for the reference ecosystem. Prefer composition
for a reusable extension. A user may subclass an ecosystem for an intentional
model-specific scientific variant, but an extension that must work across
ecosystems should not inherit from ECBBiology or access its private indices.

Illustrative BiologyModule responsibilities:

  virtual ~BiologyModule()
  module_id(), instance_id(), and schema_version()
  configure(parameter_prefix)
  requirements() and declared_reads_and_writes()
  additional_tracer_definitions() and additional_state_definitions()
  bind(resolved_field_access, state_layout)
  initialize(initialization_context, module_state)
  advance(module_context, exchange_access, module_state)
  diagnostics() and configuration_metadata()

The framework, not each module, allocates registered state and coordinates its
initialization, restart, and AMR operations. Provide module hooks only where
generic state handling is insufficient. Multiple instances need unique IDs,
parameter namespaces, and output names; an ambiguous duplicate must fail at
configuration time rather than silently share state.

An empty module list must preserve the unextended ecosystem behavior. Adding
or disabling a module is a startup configuration change, not a mid-run change
to tracer layout. The initial release need not implement a scientific module.
A small test-only exchange fixture can verify the extension contract without
introducing an oyster model or new production biology.

6. Field semantics and cross-ecosystem binding

Represent each available field by stable identity, external name, storage
location, units, elemental basis, ecological meaning, and allowed operations.
Useful meanings include particulate organic nitrogen, ammonium, dissolved
oxygen, temperature, and layer thickness. Meanings must be sufficiently
specific to distinguish detritus sizes, dissolved versus particulate pools,
and active versus diagnostic quantities.

The binding sequence is:
  1. Concrete ecosystem declares available fields and supported interactions.
  2. Each module declares required and optional inputs and exchange outputs.
  3. Explicit binding rules resolve requirements to ecosystem fields.
  4. Validate units, basis, storage location, mutability, and required options.
  5. Produce compact device-copyable index and conversion descriptors.
  6. Freeze all bindings before state allocation and timestep integration.

Simple one-to-one mappings should be straightforward. Aggregating pools,
inferring carbon from nitrogen, or distributing an exchange back among
several pools requires a documented scientific rule. Reading the sum of
multiple food pools does not define how a withdrawal should be allocated.
No binding may silently substitute zero for a missing required field.

Keep these compatibility mappings outside the reusable module's biological
equations. They may be owned by the concrete ecosystem or by explicit binding
adapters selected at setup. Ordinary module code must not switch on model
names or hard-code Bio_comp offsets. Convert metadata to fixed indices and
coefficients before launching kernels; do not search string maps on the GPU.

Illustrative compatibility example only:
  A future detritus-consuming module could bind to selected Fennel or ECB
  detritus pools, or to NEMURO PON, subject to its declared diet and elemental
  requirements. Those pools need not be scientifically equivalent. If the
  module requires explicit carbon or oxygen fields, a configuration lacking
  them is unsupported unless a separate, documented formulation supplies
  consistent semantics. A nitrogen pool alone is not an automatic carbon or
  oxygen proxy.

Reusability means the module algorithm depends on its declared contract and
an explicit compatible adapter. It does not mean every module must run with
every model or option set. Setup should report the resolved mapping or explain
precisely which requirement cannot be satisfied.

7. Exchange contract and optional persistent state

A module must be able to modify the selected ecosystem through defined
conservative exchanges, not by writing arbitrary REMORA components. Describe
each exchange by affected fields, sign, units, elemental stoichiometry, and
the state or external reservoir receiving any removed material.

The access interface may launch a fused kernel that updates bound fields;
it need not create a host-side record for every cell. The contract still
needs enough structure to check ownership, diagnose transfers, and account
for conservation. Do not introduce a generic tendency-only interface that
prevents a module from implementing its required implicit coupled equations.

Distinguish rates from increments explicitly. A rate needs integration over
the supplied duration; an already integrated exchange must not receive a
second timestep factor. Also distinguish volumetric concentrations, column
inventories, and areal boundary exchanges. For example, a bottom exchange
of amount E per unit horizontal area changes the bottom concentration by
E/Hz_bottom, with the applicable wet area and unit conversions accounted for.

If several fields represent one material removal, update the destination
and all required elemental counterparts consistently. The module's selected
equations define integration and any availability treatment; the generic
framework must not add floors, extra clipping, or a post-hoc conservation
repair. Incompatible field semantics must be rejected during binding.

Provide state descriptors for these generic cases:
  Advected water-column tracer: part of the biology scalar inventory.
  Nonadvected water-column state: separate registered 3-D state.
  Benthic or surface state: separate registered 2-D state.
  Diagnostic: derived output, not a prognostic reservoir.

For persistent state, declare units, location, initialization source, physical
transport policy, ghost-fill needs, checkpoint identity, and AMR transfer
policy. A framework that calls a module each step but does not preserve its
state is insufficient for stateful extensions.

The user-described oyster example motivates this distinction: a future module
may hold its own biomass or density, read ecosystem detritus, and return
biogeochemical exchanges. This plan supplies the lifecycle and exchange
capability only. It specifies no oyster growth equations, diet, shell
chemistry, rate constants, or oyster-specific implementation files.

The framework should handle conservative remapping for supported state kinds.
An areal density must be transferred in a way that preserves its area-integrated
amount, rather than treated as a transported volume concentration. A proposed
state kind without supported restart or AMR handling must be rejected or
explicitly restricted to the supported grid scope.

8. Numerical scheduling and exact reference mathematics

Keep the existing REMORA hook and its thickness-weighted coupling. A proposed
module-capable schedule at that hook is:

  1. Prepare ecosystem reference and working concentrations from old state,
     reproducing exactly the selected reference initialization constraints.
  2. Advance the ecosystem over dt_lev using its original internal sequence,
     BioIter policy, sinking, gas exchange, and reference constraints.
  3. Advance enabled modules over dt_lev in a documented deterministic order,
     using the resulting working ecosystem concentrations and module state.
  4. Commit the combined water-column biological increment exactly once:
       weighted_new[n] += mask * Hz * (working[n] - reference_initial[n]).
     Persist separately registered module state through its own lifecycle.

This is a proposed first-order operator split, not simultaneous coupling.
Module durations cover the same physical interval as the ecosystem. The
algorithm approximates the coupled solution with sequential operators; it
must not advance the physical clock twice. A module already integrated for
dt_lev must not be reapplied once per ecosystem BioIter.

The working state must include all enabled tracer changes consistently, and
each final contribution must be committed only once. Do not apply a base-model
increment to weighted_new, then re-add that same increment when a module
finishes. Any constraints already in the reference ecosystem belong at their
original mathematical stage, not in a new blanket post-module clip.

Prepare reference and working values for module-added tracers as well as
ecosystem tracers. Their owning formulation defines any reference constraints;
do not apply Fennel's constraints to new fields by default. The module API must
provide this preparation before its first exchange and before the final
combined increment is formed.

Start by verifying the empty-module path. If moving Fennel's existing private
scratch into the shared workspace would change operation order, first retain
the class adapter unchanged, then refactor scratch access as a separately
validated step before enabling coupled execution. Do not claim the full
module capability works until that shared-state path is implemented.

Use this split as an explicit initial coupling policy, not an assertion that
it is adequate for every extension. Strong interactions may need shared
substepping or implicit integration. Add that capability only with a specified
scheme and convergence tests. Repeatedly calling a full-step kernel with
reinitialized old-state scratch is not valid subcycling.

Expose module order and dependencies as configuration metadata. Sequential
modules see preceding changes; when multiple modules consume the same pool,
the framework must make that ordering explicit and check dependency cycles.
A simultaneous exchange solver is a separate numerical feature, not silently
equivalent to sequential updates.

BioIter<=0 requires a defined policy. Preserve the existing ecosystem-disabled
behavior. For a first module-capable release, disable the complete coupled
reaction update when its ecosystem is disabled, and report that policy.
Independent module-only stepping should be a separate supported mode, not an
accidental consequence of a Fennel-specific guard.

Strict porting rule:
  Preserve all equations, signs, process ordering, source-defined constraints,
  and time factors. Add no positivity clips, epsilon denominators, floors,
  ceilings, or bound repairs at any intermediate or final stage. Existing
  reference constraints remain part of the equations and must not be removed
  merely because they look like clipping. Any intentional scientific or
  numerical modification is a separately selected and validated variant.

For unsupported inputs or shallow-column configurations, fail clearly before
running an invalid kernel instead of inventing a modified stencil or guard.
Retain k=0..N for N+1 REMORA wet layers, N+2 interface-depth entries, and the
ROMS-to-REMORA index and unit conversions used in comparisons. REMORA's native
shortwave input is W/m2; a ROMS comparison adapter converts to its kinematic
shortwave convention exactly once.

9. Host polymorphism and GPU execution

Virtual calls select an ecosystem or module on the host at setup or update
boundaries. Each concrete implementation launches ordinary AMReX kernels
with device-copyable parameter, component, and exchange descriptors.

Do not capture a polymorphic host object, std::unique_ptr, std::vector, or
string in a device lambda. Do not require device-side vtables. Any class
functions called within a device kernel must be public and have the necessary
AMReX host/device annotations. Free device-callable helpers are also suitable.
Allocate scratch through appropriate AMReX arenas outside device work and
preserve its lifetime across asynchronous launches.

Keep whole columns available for light attenuation and sinking. A host-level
module API must also be able to launch work on the selected state location,
such as a 2-D boundary field, without assuming every extension is a 3-D tracer
reaction. Ordered launches must have correct stream dependencies and must not
race on shared ecosystem fields or outlive their binding/scratch storage.

10. Configuration, initialization, I/O, and restart

Proposed input shape, not existing syntax:
  remora.biology_model = fennel
  remora.biology_modules = <registered module instance names>
  remora.fennel.<parameter> = <value>
  remora.biology_modules.<instance>.<parameter> = <value>

Keep existing Fennel parameter names and defaults during migration. Parse
ecosystem-specific values in the concrete class and module-specific values
in their instance namespace. An empty module list is the default. Neither
ECB, NEMURO, nor a scientific module is selectable until actually implemented.

Construct a single frozen layout before allocating ncons-dependent state:
  physical fields, dyes, ecosystem tracers, additional module tracers.
Use ecosystem-first ordering to preserve legacy Fennel layouts when modules
are absent. Module-added tracer names must be unique and stable. One existing
ecosystem pool is not duplicated merely because a module requests access to
it. Store counts and component indices from the frozen descriptor list.

The model-neutral analytic initialization context supplies the selected
ecosystem identity, immutable tracer/state descriptors, grid/physical fields,
and destination views. The application remains able to define profiles.
Module initialization runs after its required physical/ecosystem fields are
available. Provide the same ordering for full-domain high-resolution ICs
before average-down. Missing required initial data is an error unless a
scientifically defined default is explicitly part of that module or model.

Reuse generic NetCDF readers and per-name boundary/river/climatology paths for
advected tracers. Additional nonadvected state needs its own declared reader
and initialization policy. Registration alone does not supply missing forcing
or a physical provider such as sediment. Fail clearly for unsatisfied inputs.

Add a biology plot group for all active advected biology fields. Preserve
fennel as an alias only for Fennel. Support selecting module prognostic state
and diagnostics by their registered output names. Attach units, location,
long name, and configuration metadata; do not force 2-D module state through
the existing 3-D scalar writer.

Restart identity must include model and schema, ordered full cons_names,
layout-defining options, module types and instance IDs, module schema/state
descriptors, resolved bindings, and coupling order. A versioned sidecar can
extend existing checkpoints with limited Header changes. Equal ncons is not
enough to accept a restart, and the same module name with different scientific
bindings can also be incompatible.

Checkpoint all registered persistent state. Define a deliberate policy for
adding/removing modules at restart; initially reject such changes without an
explicit migration. Preserve documented legacy Fennel/none restart behavior
while stating that older checkpoints cannot prove field identity. Do not
silently convert checkpoints between ecosystem or module formulations.

Record parameters and distinguish a permitted rate change from a change that
invalidates the stored layout or state semantics. The framework may need
small module-state AMR and I/O additions; keep those separate from scalar
transport changes and test their conservation and round trips.

11. Proposed implementation changes and staged delivery

Suggested additions under Source/Biology, adjusted to repository conventions:
  REMORA_BiologyBase.H
    Abstract Biology contract and common context/field descriptors.
  REMORA_BiologySystem.H/.cpp
    Ownership, factories, frozen layout, binding, update orchestration.
  REMORA_BiologyModule.H
    Generic module contract, requirements, exchanges, state descriptors.
  REMORA_Fennel.H/.cpp
    Fennel concrete class with existing parameters and reference arithmetic.
  REMORA_ECB.H/.cpp and REMORA_Nemuro.H/.cpp
    Add only as those concrete scientific kernels are implemented.

The interface and descriptor pieces can be combined initially if that keeps
the change smaller. These are not requirements to create many mostly empty
files. No oyster-specific source or input files are part of this proposal.

Targeted integration edits:
  Source/Biology/REMORA_Biology.H/.cpp
    Retain compatible external entry points and delegate to the new owner.
  Source/REMORA.H and Source/REMORA.cpp
    Store BiologySystem; query common configuration, counts, and metadata.
  Source/Initialization/REMORA_prob_common.H and REMORA_init.cpp
  Exec/REMORA_Prob.H/.cpp
    Introduce the generic IC context and temporary Fennel compatibility shim.
  Source/IO/REMORA_SetPlotVars.cpp, REMORA_NCPlotFile.cpp,
  REMORA_Checkpoint.cpp, and job-information output
    Integrate descriptors, optional state, and checkpoint identity.
  Existing level creation/regrid lifecycle entry points
    Allocate and transfer additional registered state when a module needs it.
  Source/Biology/Make.package and CMake/BuildREMORAExe.cmake
    Register new sources in both build systems.
  Existing test registration and documentation
    Add interface/binding/state tests and supported reference configurations.

Keep advance_3d's call site, physical indices, scalar advection/mixing, and
existing ncons-based transport/forcing loops unchanged by default. Limit AMR
changes to the lifecycle of additional registered state unless a demonstrated
numerical requirement demands more. Keep Fennel's reference backend and tag
format unchanged; expose backend capability through its concrete class so
another model cannot accidentally select the Fennel Fortran bridge.

Stage A: Introduce Biology and wrap Fennel with no modules.
  Exit: same configuration, field ordering, and results to roundoff, with
  no added mathematical constraints and no model switches in common callers.

Stage B: Add the optional-module capability and generic state lifecycle.
  Implement metadata, field binding, working-state exchange, deterministic
  scheduling, initialization/restart, and supported state transfer. Use a
  test-only mock exchange to demonstrate the contract. Empty modules preserve
  Stage A behavior. No production oyster module is required or proposed.

Stage C: Add a second concrete ecosystem, such as ECB.
  Pin the scientific reference and port its equations exactly. Exercise the
  same test-only module contract with a second explicit compatible adapter;
  otherwise a Fennel-only mock would not demonstrate cross-model binding.

Stage D: Validate coupled execution and extend supported configurations.
  Test timestep convergence of the coupling policy, input/output paths,
  parallel execution, restart, and supported AMR state kinds. Introduce a
  real scientific extension only in separate user work or a separate PR.

Stage E: Optional scientific subclasses and advanced coupling.
  A concrete-model subclass can represent a deliberate scientific variant
  behind the same interface. Common reusable extensions should still use
  BiologyModule composition rather than duplicating that variant for every
  ecosystem. Shared chemistry/sinking helpers and advanced coupled solvers
  require separate evidence that their mathematical contracts match.

12. Validation and acceptance criteria

Reference ecosystem agreement:
  Compare matched ROMS and REMORA output to roundoff under the same initial
  conditions, forcing, parameter values, option set, and output timing.
  Specify tolerances consistent with precision and diagnosed arithmetic
  differences; do not relax tolerances to hide a changed equation. Ensure
  tested variables/processes respond rather than remaining identically zero
  or acting as no-ops. Functional/passive pools need nonuniform transport or
  another relevant check, not an invented reaction.

  Use the existing guidance in Source/Biology/Fortran/README.md and tag_map.md
  when the Fennel bridge or detailed diagnostics are needed. Preserve that
  established protocol. A new per-ecosystem Fortran bridge and comparisons at
  every internal stage are optional debugging aids, not automatic acceptance
  gates when meaningful output agreement already verifies the port.

Empty-module and disabled-module behavior:
  Fennel with no extensions reproduces the baseline. A disabled test module
  changes no state or layout unexpectedly. Biology-none and BioIter-disabled
  policies are explicit. Unsupported backend requests fail rather than using
  a different ecosystem's reference implementation.

Interface and binding:
  No hard-coded ecosystem enum branches inside the test module. Verify its
  required inputs and exchanges through two compatible concrete-model field
  adapters. Reject missing oxygen/carbon, wrong units, duplicate names, or
  unsupported write access. Test nonzero dye counts so Bio_comp is not assumed
  to equal Tracer_comp. Validate frozen-layout counts and name/index agreement.

Exchange and coupling:
  Use a minimal test-only conservative transfer between represented pools or
  a registered reservoir. Verify equal accounted removal and addition, correct
  areal/volumetric conversion, signs, stoichiometry, and no duplicate timestep
  or increment application. Use nonuniform Hz and a nonzero incoming weighted
  state. A no-change update must yield exactly zero increment under the
  reference constraint convention. The framework adds no clipping.

  Test deterministic order, shared-pool interactions, conflicting dependencies,
  and expected first-order convergence for the proposed sequential split.
  Scientific module implementations must supply their own equation-specific
  tests when introduced; the generic fixture is not scientific validation of
  an oyster or other production module.

Persistent state and I/O:
  Exercise supported 2-D and 3-D state registration, initialization, ghost fills,
  checkpoint/restart, and regridding. Compare restarted and uninterrupted runs.
  Reject equal-count but different-layout/binding/module checkpoints. Check
  high-resolution initialization and average-down ordering. Confirm both
  advected and nonadvected outputs retain units and storage locations.

Budgets and parallel portability:
  For domain inventories, sum over valid wet cells excluding coarse cells
  covered by finer levels. Use concentration times Hz times horizontal area
  for volume pools and state times area for areal densities. Include external
  losses, burial, and other represented reservoirs. Do not double-count
  chlorophyll as an independent elemental inventory.
  Check CPU/MPI and native GPU execution with justified roundoff expectations,
  public device-callable helpers, intact columns, valid scratch lifetimes,
  and absence of concurrent writes. The host-only Fennel bridge is not a GPU
  implementation or a universal backend for all models/modules.

13. Decisions to settle before implementation

Agree on the abstract interface and context names, field semantic descriptors,
the first supported exchange/state kinds, and the generic IC migration. Pick
the second ecosystem reference/options and identify which optional fields it
can actually provide to modules. Specify the initial coupling order, disabled
reaction policy, and restart compatibility rules.

The immediate deliverable is a reference-preserving Biology abstraction plus
the capability to attach optional modules through declared dependencies,
explicit field adapters, controlled exchanges, and a complete state lifecycle.
It is not a rewrite of all biology kernels, an automatic interchange of
different scientific pools, or the implementation of a new oyster module.
